# SYNOPSIS

```text
/dev/sdXX/ctl
/dev/sdXX/data
/dev/sdXX/NAME
```

# DESCRIPTION

Each disk is a directory, as Plan 9's sd(3) has it: a disk service, or a driver of the `block` class, serves it, named for its kind and unit. ramdisk(4)'s is `sdZ0`; devmgr(8) binds a driver's at `/dev/sd`<letter><n>, virtio-blk(3)'s at `/dev/sdF0` and on, nvme(3)'s at `/dev/sdN0` and on.

: `ctl`
  Reads as Plan 9's: `inquiry` and the disk's name, `geometry` with the number of sectors and the sector size, `alignment` with the sector size and 0, and a `part` <name> <start> <end> line for each partition, `data` first, <end> the sector after its last.
  It takes `flush`: what was written and answered before it is made durable (the device's cache flushed); on a disk with no cache, it is answered at once.
: `data`
  The whole disk, its length the disk's.
: <name>
  A partition, from the disk's GUID partition table, read when the disk is first served: a file named by the partition's name.
  A partition whose name a file can't have, or with none, is `part`<n>, its entry's number; a second of one name, or one named `data` or `ctl`, has the number after it.
  A disk with no GUID partition table has only `data`.

A read of `data` or a partition may start anywhere and be any length; it is cut at the partition's end.
A write must be whole sectors, at a sector's start, or it fails with `bad arg in system call`; one that runs past the partition's end is cut short there, and one at or past it writes nothing.
On a read-only disk a write fails with `permission denied`.
Each read and write is answered when the disk has done it, so a `flush` after them covers them all.

`data` and each partition take a ring (node(5)), as a client moving its bulk attaches: each entry is a request to the disk, and entries are answered in the order the disk ends them.

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% cat /dev/sdF0/ctl
inquiry virtio-blk
geometry 131072 512
alignment 512 0
part data 0 131072
part taisce 2048 131039
% echo flush >/dev/sdF0/ctl
```

